=== constructor vs cloud request ===
Computer constructor=(*, provider: 'ProviderName | None' = None, local: 'bool | None' = None, lifetime: "Literal['ephemeral', 'persistent']" = 'ephemeral', **options: 'Any') -> 'None'
CloudProvider constructor=(*, api_key: 'str | None' = None, base_url: 'str' = 'https://api.celesto.ai', organization_id: 'str | None' = None, startup_timeout: 'float' = 120, cleanup_timeout: 'float' = 120, vcpus: 'int | None' = None, ram_mb: 'int | None' = None, disk_size_mb: 'int | None' = None, image: 'str' = 'ubuntu-desktop-24.04', template_id: 'str | None' = None, template_version: 'str | None' = None, external_volume_enabled: 'bool' = False) -> 'None'
Computer lifetime is a wrapper lifecycle flag; CloudProvider constructor has no lifetime parameter.
CloudProvider request model includes size_id and network_policy, but CloudProvider constructor does not expose either.
CloudProvider constructor does expose external_volume_enabled, vcpus, ram_mb, disk_size_mb, image, template_id, template_version.
NetworkPolicyMode serialized_values=OFF:off,OPEN:open
